Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.230 align:start position:0%
 
hi there steve kaufman  um let me just get this centered better  today was a great day here um  i went skiing cross-country skiing in  vancouver  uh it was raining  last couple of days and the temperature  dropped and so there was lots of snow up  here in the mountains so 20 minutes from  where i live  i went from a rainy weather to beautiful  snow  had a great time  and i have my frozen shoulder and uh at  least i'm able to cross-country ski so  that makes me a little happier otherwise  my activities have been really cut back  i can't play hockey i can't do anything  but uh today was great  now i want to talk uh in response to a  comment that kevin made on a previous  post  uh  where i spoke i think i commented about  where i  made reference to a site that referred  to english people not speaking languages  although a number of them said they  would like to  and kevin said well yeah but the english  are not the only ones that are  monolingual and which is true but of  course they're more monolingual  than the others like uh when i was in  italy all kinds of people spoke english  portugal the same way  but it's not really fair to compare  because really the english i mean  everyone speaks english so there is less  incentive for them to speak english  and kevin then sent me  in the direction of an article by a mark  morgenfelder i think it was and i've  posted a connection to or a link to his  article  where he explains all the reasons why  people don't want to learn  languages and he says that it's a very  difficult it's an unpleasant thing to do  and people only do it if they have to  so i want to talk about that because i  want to compare language learning to  golf now i could compare language  learning to sudoku or learning to play a  musical instrument or any other  thing  hobby activity that people to do people  do because they like to do those  activities  and  to me and i  you know yeah i'm a bit of a language  nut  uh  but  uh  language learning  should be an enjoyable thing to do  and i  am of the belief  and that's largely behind my motivation  and link  i'm of the belief that more people don't  learn languages because it is made out  to be an obligation something that we  have to do and so forth and so on  now if you look at golf if you compare  golf  to  language learning i mean language  learning is much more pleasant  you are far more likely to succeed  you're far less likely to be frustrated  it's a lot less expensive  uh and it's better for you it's better  for your brain  and i mean you look at golf and i play  golf i'm not as keen as my wife but i do  play golf i went through a period where  i tried very hard to improve  i realized that no matter how hard i  practiced i wasn't going to get any  better that every time i go out to play  golf there's going to be x number of  times when i'll hit that ball and it  won't go anywhere near where i wanted it  to go  so you know four and a half five hours  walking around the golf course and  increasingly you don't even get to walk  you have to ride in a cart  so there's very little exercise  lots of frustration it's expensive it's  50 60 70 80 if you go to an expensive  course 100 150  for the privilege of going around and  being frustrated and most people never  improve i mean the young people improve  and then they  you know the older people most of the  people who take it up are older and they  very quickly reach their level and they  never ever improve  uh yeah it's social me with friends  whatever that's true have a beer after  the game and all that but  if i compare it to language learning  language learning is very good stimulus  for the brain if you do it right you're  actually doing interesting things  listening to interesting things reading  interesting things  you're able to track your improvement  and again at length you know you're  gradually seeing a number of words that  you know increase it's actually  cumulative golf isn't cumulative you can  play well one day practice the whole  week and play worse the following week  there is no sense of of an accumulation  whereas in language learning you do  accumulate words  uh the other thing is you know you can  learn languages while doing exercises  that are far more physical exercise  that's far more strenuous than golf i do  it all the time when i was up skiing  cross-country skiing in the snow i was  listening to russian  my my wife gets furious because i will  play golf listening to languages  when i i can be exercising lifting  weights doing my abdominal exercises  listening to languages i can be walking  listening to languages  so it's healthy it's good for the brain  and at the end of it  you actually end up being able to speak  in the language  okay you end up with something  something's left  so  to me yeah there's all kinds of  hobbies that that are out there and not  everyone's going to like the same thing  and i i have always said  i don't consider someone who isn't  interested in languages a worse person  you know there's lots of multilingual  people who i don't like and there's lots  of unilingual people that i like it  doesn't make you a better or worse  person but i think we can do a much  better job  of making language learning an  interesting and fun activity for its own  sake just the pleasure of listening to a  different language  and  in fact this article by this mark  fellow  there's a lot that i agree with in what  he says about things that are not true  about language learning but the idea  that language learning has to be a  utilitarian  activity that you only do it if you have  to i just i think that's true for a lot  of people maybe even be true for the  majority of people  but people do lots of things that are  not utilitarian people play golf which  has no purpose and it's expensive and  frustrating  people do sudoku which has no purpose so  can we not structure language learning  in such a way that it is a fun thing to  do for its own sake the activity of  learning of listening is fun  and you get the added benefit that you  now have a language which will enable  you to communicate with people whether  it be socially or for business or for  watching movies or for reading books you  now acquire this  other door  to a part of humanity you know that has  been kind of off limits  for you so that you have these  tremendous benefits and in that sense  i think it's really unfortunate  that this whole utilitarian approach to  language learning  you know and you've got to learn it as  fast as possible and do it and be done  with it no no just do it and enjoy it  and  don't just do english or now chinese or  spanish  there used to be far more  interest in a variety of languages  language exchanges to germany to japan  to portugal to i don't know  kyrgyzstan  it doesn't matter it's wherever you go  and you meet up with people of a  different culture and you learn their  language if it's done  well learning that language can be fun  and you end up with the advantage that  you now can  interact  in a much richer way with the people and  the culture of a different language so  my take on it is i would far rather  go  cross-country skiing by the way i went  cross-country skiing today and listened  to echo mesquite my russian my wife  played golf if you ask me what i would  rather do  the proof is where i went today and  where she went today so  let us continue to find ways to make  language learning a fun thing to do not  just  uh utilitarian activity so that's my  take on language learning and golf  thank you

